Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General Hospital  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Tel  617  REMOVED_DATE    Molecular Pathology Report  HFE SNaPshot Analysis      Accession     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  J   DOB  REMOVED_DATE  Age  35   Sex  F  MRN   REMOVED_CASE_ID   Institution  MGH  Location  CPZ 501  BEACON HILL PRIM CARE  Date of Collection  REMOVED_DATE  Date of Accession  REMOVED_DATE  Reported  REMOVED_DATE 17 47  Results To   Erika Hyde Riley MD  MPH      CLINICAL HISTORY   Elevated liver function tests  rule out hereditary Hemochromatosis    SPECIMENS RECEIVED   A  Molecular test for HFE    SLIDE BLOCK DESCRIPTION   Blood            TEST   HFE Gene Mutation Analysis   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      INDICATION FOR TEST  Evaluate for Hereditary Hemochromatosis    SPECIMEN S  TESTED  Blood    RESULTS     NEGATIVE FOR  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  MUTATION    NEGATIVE FOR  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  MUTATION    INTERPRETATION   This patient does not carry the mutations most clearly associated with  hereditary hemochromatosis  Published reports indicate this does not completely  rule out a genetic basis of hemochromatosis since 5 10  of such patients do not  harbor these mutations  this percentage is much higher in non Caucasian  populations     TEST INFORMATION   Hereditary hemochromatosis is an autosomal recessive disease of iron overload  affecting 1 in REMOVED_DATE individuals of Northern European descent 1   The HFE gene  mutations 845G  A   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   cysteine to tyrosine at amino acid 282  and 187C  G    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   histidine to aspartic acid at amino acid 63  have been found in a  majority of hereditary hemochromatosis cases in Caucasian populations 1   The  identification of the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  homozygous mutation is most closely associated with  hereditary hemochromatosis and with elevated ferritin and transferrin saturation  levels 2   The importance of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  heterozygous mutations is controversial   including compound  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutants  as is the presence of homozygous or  heterozygous  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutations 1  3   especially in the setting of secondary causes  of iron overload  Of note   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  heterozygosity is present in up to 10  of the  general population  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  heterozygosity in up to 24  2   The presence of HFE   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  alleles was determined using DNA extracted from peripheral blood  with single base extension genotyping technology  This assay will not detect  rare mutations in the HFE gene that may be associated with hereditary  hemochromatosis  Correlation of genetic findings with clinical and laboratory  findings is required to make a diagnosis of hereditary hemochromatosis     REFERENCES   1  Lyon E  Frank EL  Hereditary hemochromatosis since discovery of the HFE  gene  Clin Chem  REMOVED_CASE_ID  47 7   REMOVED_CASE_ID  56   2  Adams PC  Reboussin DM  Barton JC  McLaren CE  Eckfeldt JH  McLaren GD   Dawkins FW  Acton RT  Harris EL  Gordeuk VR  Leiendecker Foster C  Speechley M   Snively BM  Holup JL  Thomson E  Sholinsky P  Hemochromatosis and iron overload  screening in a racially diverse population  N Engl J Med  REMOVED_CASE_ID  352 17   REMOVED_CASE_ID  78   3  Olynyk JK  Cullen DJ  Aquilia S  Rossi E  Summerville L  Powell LW  A  population based study of the clinical expression of the hemochromatosis gene  N  Engl J Med  REMOVED_CASE_ID  341 10  718 24     This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Center for Integrated Diagnostics  It has not been approved by the U S   Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that such clearance is not  necessary  This test is used for clinical purposes  Pursuant to the requirements  of CLIA 88  